---
title: "Get Started"
description: "Create a SvelteKit app, add the PlainSv styles, types, and app context with the init item, then copy in Svelte 5 components, utilities, and plugins that need few external packages."
---

# PlainSv

PlainSv is a collection of accessible Svelte 5 components that follow the shadcn design, written the Svelte way. Each component keeps its state in runes, its styles in a scoped `<style>` block, and builds on native elements such as `dialog` where the browser already does the work, with no Tailwind, no PostCSS setup, and no headless UI library underneath. Alongside the components, PlainSv ships [utilities](/docs/utilities) for the rest of a Svelte app, such as CSS effects, typed server functions, and Vite plugins for Markdown pages and icon imports. PlainSv is not a package you install from npm: each item's source is copied into your project with the `remote0` CLI, so you own the code and can change anything you need.

## Create a project

To get started with PlainSv, create a TypeScript SvelteKit project with the `sv` CLI. Replace `project-name` with the name of your project, and pick the adapter for the host you deploy to when `sv` asks, such as `node`, `vercel`, `cloudflare`, or `static`.

```pm
npx sv@latest create project-name
```

Optionally, delete the default files and folders that PlainSv does not use.

```bash title="Terminal"
rm src/lib/index.ts && rm -rf src/lib/assets
```

## Add init

Inside the new project, add the `init` item.

```pm
npx remote0 add https://plainsv.com/r/init.json
```

The `init` item adds the PlainSv core files and installs their packages:

- Add the theme, resets, CSS utilities, and typeset styles in `src/styles`
- Replace `src/app.d.ts` with the global types that components use
- Add the app context in `src/lib/context/app.svelte.ts` and the app data in `src/lib/data/app.ts`
- Add the `Logo` and `PageMeta` components in `src/lib/components/shared`
- Replace `vite.config.ts` with a config that registers the dev and svx plugins and compiles `.svx` pages
- Replace `src/routes/+layout.svelte` with a root layout that imports the styles, creates the app context, and renders toasts
- Add `src/routes/+layout.server.ts`, which passes the theme and device to the app context
- Add `src/hooks.server.ts`, which sets dark mode on the server so pages never flash light
- Add the favicon, icons, Open Graph image, `manifest.json`, and `robots.txt` in `static`
- Add `.env` and `.env.production` with `DOMAIN_URL`
- Add `.vscode/settings.json`, which sorts the VS Code explorer by file type so each component's `.svelte` parts sit together, apart from its `.ts` files
- Add the `toaster` component and the `dev-plugin` and `svx-plugin-simple` Vite plugins

Since `init` replaces `src/app.d.ts`, `vite.config.ts`, and `src/routes/+layout.svelte`, `remote0` asks before it overwrites them. Copy anything you added to those files back in afterward, and see the adapter notes below.

## Vite config

There is nothing to set up here, since `init` writes `vite.config.ts` with the dev and svx plugins already registered and `.svx` pages enabled. Update it only if your project needs something different. It uses `adapter-node`, so if `sv` picked another adapter, change the `adapter` import to the one `sv` installed, such as `@sveltejs/adapter-vercel`. You can also add your own plugins or compiler options. This is the file `init` writes.

```ts title="vite.config.ts"
import adapter from "@sveltejs/adapter-node";
import devPlugin from "#lib/server/plugins/dev/index.js";
import svxPlugin from "#lib/server/plugins/svx/index.js";
import { sveltekit } from "@sveltejs/kit/vite";
import { defineConfig } from "vite";

export default defineConfig(() => {
	return {
		plugins: [
			devPlugin(),
			svxPlugin(),
			sveltekit({
				adapter: adapter(),
				extensions: [".svelte", ".svx"],
				compilerOptions: {
					// Force runes mode for the project, except for libraries. Can be removed in svelte 6.
					runes: ({ filename }) => (filename.split(/[/\\]/).includes("node_modules") ? undefined : true),
					// Allow reading props once, such as `setAppCtx(data)` in the root layout
					warningFilter: (warning) => (warning.code === "state_referenced_locally" ? false : true),
				},
			}),
		],
	};
});
```

## Scripts recommendation

When using the node adapter on Node 26 or later, we recommend this setup. `--env-file-if-exists` loads `.env` natively without a package, and the preview script also loads `.env.production`.

It also runs the dev and preview servers through [portless](https://github.com/vercel-labs/portless), which gives each one a stable named URL such as `https://project-name.localhost` instead of a port number you have to remember. portless assigns a free port through `$PORT`, which the scripts pass to Vite with `--strictPort`, and the `.prod` suffix gives the preview server its own URL so it can run beside the dev server. Replace `project-name` with your project's name.

```pm
npm install -g portless
```

Set the scripts as follows.

```json title="package.json"
{
	"vite:dev": "node --env-file-if-exists=.env ./node_modules/vite/bin/vite.js",
	"build": "node --env-file-if-exists=.env ./node_modules/vite/bin/vite.js build",
	"vite:preview": "node --env-file-if-exists=.env --env-file-if-exists=.env.production ./node_modules/vite/bin/vite.js preview",
	"dev": "portless project-name sh -c 'pnpm vite:dev --port $PORT --strictPort --host ${HOST:-127.0.0.1}'",
	"preview": "portless project-name.prod sh -c 'pnpm vite:preview --port $PORT --strictPort --host ${HOST:-127.0.0.1}'",
	"prepare": "svelte-kit sync || echo ''",
	"check": "svelte-kit sync && svelte-check --tsconfig ./tsconfig.json",
	"check:watch": "svelte-kit sync && svelte-check --tsconfig ./tsconfig.json --watch"
}
```

## Root layout and dark mode

The root layout imports `app.css` once so every page has the theme tokens, creates the app context from the layout data, and renders the toasts. Components render without color, spacing, or radius until these styles load. Render `PageMeta` in each page to set its title, description, and Open Graph tags.

```svelte title="src/routes/+page.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { PageMeta } from "#lib/components/shared/index.js";
	import { useAppCtx } from "#lib/context/app.svelte.js";

	const app = useAppCtx();
</script>

<PageMeta title="Home" />

<button onclick={() => app.toggleDarkMode()}>Toggle theme</button>
```

The app context saves the theme in a `theme` cookie. `src/hooks.server.ts` reads the cookie and adds the `dark` class to `body` before the page is sent, and `src/routes/+layout.server.ts` passes it to the app context with `isMobile` and `userData`.

## Adapter notes

The `src/app.d.ts` that `init` writes works with the `auto`, `node`, `bun`, `vercel`, and `static` adapters. A few adapters need one more step.

For Cloudflare, `init` replaces the `App.Platform` types that `sv` added, so add them back inside `namespace App` in `src/app.d.ts`, then run `npx wrangler types` to generate the `Env` type in `worker-configuration.d.ts`.

```ts title="src/app.d.ts"
declare global {
	namespace App {
		// Cloudflare bindings, request context, and cache, typed by `wrangler types`
		interface Platform {
			env: Env;
			ctx: ExecutionContext;
			caches: CacheStorage;
			cf?: IncomingRequestCfProperties;
		}
	}
}
```

For a static site, set `prerender` to `true` in `src/routes/+layout.server.ts`. Prerendered pages are built once, so the hook cannot read the theme cookie, and dark mode applies after the page loads.

For Bun, add `bunfig.toml` so package scripts such as `vite build` run on the Bun runtime, which `adapter-bun` requires.

```toml title="bunfig.toml"
[run]
bun = true
```

## Run the app

Start the dev server, then open `http://localhost:5173`.

```pm
npm run dev
```

## Make it yours

The `init` item ships with placeholder content. Replace it with your own before you deploy.

Update `src/lib/data/app.ts` with your app's name, title, and description. The page meta reads these values. Update the same name and description in `static/manifest.json` for the web app manifest, and the `name` field in `package.json`.

```ts title="src/lib/data/app.ts"
/** App core data */
export const appData = {
	name: "Acme",
	title: "Acme, the app that does it all",
	description: "A short sentence that describes your app.",
};
```

Replace the images in `static/images` with your own and keep the same file names: `favicon.svg`, `apple-icon.png`, `manifest-192.png`, `manifest-512.png`, and `og.png`. The page meta uses the favicon, the Apple icon, and the Open Graph image, and the web app manifest uses the two manifest icons. Then swap the SVG in `src/lib/components/shared/logo.svelte` for your own logo.

Set `DOMAIN_URL` in `.env.production` to the URL where your app will run. The `.env` file keeps `http://localhost:3000` for local development. A server build will not start without `DOMAIN_URL`, so also set it as an environment variable on your host, or under `vars` in `wrangler.jsonc` for Cloudflare.

```bash title=".env.production"
DOMAIN_URL="https://acme.com"
```

## Installation

Add a component with the `remote0` CLI and its registry URL. The source files are copied into your project at the same paths they have here.

```pm
npx remote0 add https://plainsv.com/r/button.json
```

Replace `button` with the component name, which matches the last part of its docs URL, such as `card` or `dropdown-menu`. Components that build on other components install those too. Components expect the global types and the styles, which the `init` item installs, so add `init` before your first component.

## Theme

Components read their colors, radii, spacing, and font sizes from the CSS variables in `src/styles/theme.css`, which the `init` item installs. The global `src/styles/app.css` imports the theme, the CSS utilities, and the typeset styles, and the root layout imports `app.css` once so every page has the tokens.

```css title="src/styles/app.css"
@import "./theme.css";
@import "./utils.css";
@import "./typeset.css";
```

The theme defines a light palette on `:root` and a dark palette that applies when the `dark` class is set. Override any token to restyle every component at once.

```css title="src/styles/theme.css"
:root {
	--radius: 0.625rem;
	--primary: oklch(0.205 0 0);
	--primary-foreground: oklch(0.985 0 0);
}
```

## CSS utilities

A few components, such as `ai-action` and `prompt-input`, use the `shimmer` and `scroll-fade` classes from `src/styles/utils.css`. The `init` item installs the file, and `app.css` imports it after the theme so the classes work on every page.

```svelte title="+page.svelte"
<span class="shimmer">Generating response...</span>

<ul class="list scroll-fade-y">
	<!-- Long list -->
</ul>
```

Read more about the classes and their variables on the [CSS utilities page](/docs/utilities/css).

## Global types

Components share the `UiSize` and `UiRounded` types, the `Snippet` and `Component` types, and the `CompProps` helpers that type props from an HTML element. The `init` item declares them globally in `src/app.d.ts`, so components can use them without imports.

```ts title="src/app.d.ts"
import type { Snippet as SvSnippet, Component as SvComponent, ComponentProps as SvComponentProps } from "svelte";
import type { SvelteHTMLElements } from "svelte/elements";

declare global {
	type Snippet<T extends unknown[] = []> = SvSnippet<T>;
	type Component = SvComponent;
	type ComponentProps<T> = SvComponentProps<T>;
	type CompAttrs<TagName extends keyof HTMLElementTagNameMap> = Omit<SvelteHTMLElements[TagName], `on${string}` | `bind:${string}` | "children">;
	type CompEvents<TagName extends keyof HTMLElementTagNameMap> = { [K in keyof HTMLElementTagNameMap[TagName] as K extends `on${string}` ? K : never]?: (e: any) => any };
	type CompProps<TagName extends keyof HTMLElementTagNameMap> = CompAttrs<TagName> & CompEvents<TagName>;
	type CompPropsWithChild<TagName extends keyof HTMLElementTagNameMap> = CompProps<TagName> & { children: SvSnippet };
	type CompPropsWithChildSimple<ExtraT> = { children: SvSnippet } & ExtraT;
	type UiSize = "xs" | "sm" | "md" | "lg";
	type UiRounded = "xs" | "sm" | "md" | "lg" | "xl" | "2xl" | "3xl" | "4xl" | "pill";
}

export {};
```

## Using a component

Each component lives in its own folder with a `_.svelte` file and an `index.ts` file that exports the component and its props type. Import components from the folder's `index.js`.

```svelte title="+page.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { Button } from "#lib/components/ui/button/index.js";
</script>

<Button>Sign in</Button>
```

## Sizes and corners

Sized components accept `size` and `rounded` props. When `rounded` is omitted, the corner radius follows the size.

```svelte title="+page.svelte"
<Button size="sm">Small</Button>
<Button size="lg" rounded="pill">Large pill</Button>
```

## Variants

Components that have visual styles accept a `variant` prop with the shadcn names `default`, `secondary`, `outline`, `ghost`, `destructive`, and `link`.

```svelte title="+page.svelte"
<Button variant="outline">Cancel</Button>
<Button variant="destructive">Delete</Button>
```

## Styling

Components pass extra attributes through to the root element, so the recommended way to override one is the `style` attribute. They also render their props as `data-variant`, `data-size`, and `data-rounded` attributes on the root element.

```svelte title="+page.svelte"
<Button style="margin-top: 1rem">Save</Button>
```

Read more about inline styles, theme tokens, other ways to override, and new variants on the [styling page](/docs/styling).

## Server functions

Add type-safe server functions with the `functions` item. Define a function's input and output with Zod in `src/lib/functions/schemas.ts`, export a handler with the same name from `src/lib/functions/handlers`, and call it from the client with `useFunc`.

```pm
npx remote0 add https://plainsv.com/r/functions.json
```

```svelte title="+page.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { useFunc } from "#lib/functions/index.js";

	const response = await useFunc("userCreate", { email: "ada@example.com" });
</script>
```

Read more about validation, private functions, uploads, and streaming on the [functions page](/docs/utilities/functions). The [utilities page](/docs/utilities) also covers the Vite plugins that compile `.svx` pages and speed up icon imports.

## Browse components

Browse every component and its demos on the [components page](/docs/components), and see them working together in full-screen apps on the [examples page](/examples).

## For LLMs

Every component page is also available as plain Markdown by adding `.md` to its URL, such as `/docs/components/button.md`. The [llms.txt](/llms.txt) file lists them all.

